WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.490
Both the iPhone XS Max and the new Samsung
Galaxy S10 are incredibly powerful phones,

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.339
so powerful that they’ve surpassed what
even the best games and apps available on

00:00:09.339 --> 00:00:11.730
either app store can take advantage of.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.240
So is there really a point to run speed test
comparisons anymore?

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.240
Who cares.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:17.240
Let’s do it anyway.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.480
We’re gonna throw some benchmarks in at
the end, but we’re gonna focus on tests

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.759
that you’ll notice in real life, unlike
the unrealistic RAM management tests we see

00:00:24.759 --> 00:00:26.199
all over YouTube.

00:00:26.199 --> 00:00:29.310
Let’s start with which phone can boot up
quicker.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:34.160
The iPhone hits the lock screen only slightly
quicker than the Samsung, but after unlocking

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.850
them, we saw that the S10 was still booting,
which is surprising.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:41.920
Next, let’s see which one loads and plays
a YouTube video first.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.401
The S10 started playing considerably quicker,
and this is actually something you’ll notice

00:00:46.401 --> 00:00:47.989
in day-to-day use.

00:00:47.989 --> 00:00:52.449
We tested this multiple times and the S10
was quicker basically every time.

00:00:52.449 --> 00:00:56.820
Next up, let’s load some of the most common
apps people use, like Instagram.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.420
Again, the S10 finished quite a bit sooner.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.949
Now with Facebook, we see the same result
once again.

00:01:03.949 --> 00:01:07.210
So far, we’re really impressed with the
speed of the S10.

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:10.380
Snapchat is used pretty often, so let’s
test that.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.770
And there you have it, that’s the nail in
the coffin.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:17.060
The Samsung is consistently loading apps quicker
than the XS Max.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:22.760
Now let’s test LTE speed, both using T-Mobile’s
unlimited LTE network in our office.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:26.490
Google’s internet speed test shows the S10
to be slightly faster.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.579
Let’s see what Ooklas speed test shows.

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:35.780
Surprisingly, the XS Max got around 10Mbps
faster in this test, so we reran the test

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:41.140
and found that the S10 was now faster, so
we’re not sure how accurate this app is.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.020
Next up, let’s download some games and see
which one finished faster.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:49.610
Starting with Fruit Ninja, the S10 seems to
be pending for quite a while before it actually

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:50.700
starts downloading.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.509
Due to this, the XS Max finishes quite a bit
sooner.

00:01:53.509 --> 00:01:54.509
Let’s try another one.

00:01:54.509 --> 00:01:59.320
Now downloading Toon Blast, the S10 is doing
the same thing again, and it ends up taking

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.070
quite a while longer to download and install.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.869
Now let’s see which phone loads web pages
quicker.

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:08.500
In the first run, the S10 was a good amount
quicker to load.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:12.320
This is something you’ll experience every
day, so we’re pretty impressed.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:17.490
Surprisingly, on the second run, the XS Max
loaded the images quicker, even though it

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.850
seemed to start loading later.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:22.640
Loading Apple.com is quicker on the S10.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.760
Same with loading the iPhone XR product page,
and the XS Product page as well.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.990
Overall, the S10 loads web pages noticeably
quicker.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:35.480
Now let’s see which phone loads some games
quicker, starting with everyone’s favorite,

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:36.480
Fortnite.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.850
Surprisingly, the iPhone XS Max finished 16
seconds sooner than the S10, which will easily

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.480
make a difference for gamers.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:46.760
Let’s try some smaller games, like Clash
Royale.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:50.340
Both phones loaded this one at basically the
exact same time.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:55.080
Now in Clash of Clans, the S10 finished quicker,
but not much at all, so you won’t really

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:56.900
notice a difference in the real-world.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.330
We also decided to test camera shutter speed,
and there’s a considerable amount of lag

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:07.390
while using the S10, which is pretty unfortunate
since the XS Max snaps a photo instantly.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:10.440
This may be a big deal for some, and that’s
definitely me.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.280
Now let’s finish off with some benchmarks,
starting with Geekbench 4.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.191
From the looks of it, there’s not much of
a difference in raw multi-core power, but

00:03:17.191 --> 00:03:19.600
a great difference in single-core.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.100
Considering that the XS Max has half the RAM,
it’s pretty impressive.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:28.260
In the popular Antutu graphics benchmark,
the S10 actually beats the XS Max by about

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.740
25,000 points, which is really impressive.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.970
We’ve seen scores as high as 350,000 on
the iPhone, but they may have updated the

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:34.970
app recently.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:36.930
In Antutu’s HTML5 test, the XS Max beats
out the S10, but it doesn’t match the results

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.980
we saw earlier in browser speed.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:45.940
Finally, in GFXBench’s famous 1080P Manhattan
offscreen test, the iPhone scored around 119

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:50.880
FPS compared to 102 on the S10, which are
both great scores.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.080
Overall, we’re really impressed with the
Galaxy S10+.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.880
It scores very high in benchmarks and it seems
to load apps that we all use in day-to-day

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.670
life just a little bit quicker than the iPhone.

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:06.660
In some cases, the XS Max was quicker, like
loading Fortnite, but that’s about it.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:10.220
Either way, both phones are incredibly quick
and it’ll be hard to notice a difference

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:14.630
in real life, but from our testing, the S10
is definitely the winner here.

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:19.070
We’re gonna be testing more things like
the cameras, battery life and gaming performance

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:23.530
in Fortnite, so if you’re not already subscribed,
click that button right there to sub now!

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:30.410
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next video!

